    {
      "card_ids": ["C23"],
      "claim": "Abecarnil, a large lipophilic fused heteroaromatic ester, had absolute oral bioavailability of 39±17% in healthy volunteers, supporting F≥20% despite high lipophilicity and aromaticity.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C15"],
      "claim": "Alfuzosin, an amino-substituted fused heteroaromatic with methoxy groups, had absolute oral bioavailability of 64.5% in healthy subjects, supporting high F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C51"],
      "claim": "Betrixaban, a high-molecular-weight polar heteroaromatic drug, had absolute oral bioavailability of 47% in humans, supporting that complex structures can exceed 20%.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "All transferable human oral bioavailability data from structurally related heteroaromatic drugs (piritrexim, trimazosin, abecarnil, alfuzosin, betrixaban) are ≥20%, mostly 34-77%, supporting high oral bioavailability for the query. The physicochemical prior of low F is outweighed by consistent experimental evidence, though low Morgan similarities and lack of an exact analogue lower confidence.",
